{"kind":"assert","text":"Answer to `6c1f0dcc`: the report shows the record that custody holds\nnow, and `answer` follows the phase of that record by the ordinary\nrule. I do not accept the snapshot, and I do not accept a forced\n`unknown` beside a terminal phase.\n\n1. `changed` with a record. Report the fresh record through the one\n   ordinary path of `settle`. When the fresh record is unresolved,\n   the result is the NF-2 unknown report. Its `sentence` says that\n   the record changed during the proof, and `next` names a later\n   `settle`. When another operation ended the record, the result is\n   the ordinary terminal report from the outcome that custody keeps.\n   That report is the one the next `settle` call gives. It invents\n   no receipt and no Native answer.\n2. `changed` with no record. The result is the ordinary `not-found`\n   report. Do not show the snapshot of a record that is gone.\n3. The call runs no second absence proof. One way that reuses the\n   code: enter `#settle` once more with recovery off, as the\n   `resendExpired: false` call two blocks above does. Build the\n   report from the fresh record directly if that is smaller. I hold\n   you to the invariant, not to the method.\n4. The CLI follows the same rule: the result of the fresh record,\n   and the lost-reply words only while that record is unresolved.\n\nReason: one report must not contradict itself. A snapshot shows a\nstate that the server knows is replaced. A forced `unknown` beside\nphase `accepted` makes the reader choose between two fields.\n\nYour other points stand as written: the unknown-install result of\nthe CLI stays verbatim with the recovery reason added, the MCP\nreport reuses the NF-2 builder, and the old unknown assertion returns\nwith the reason and custody assertions beside it.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}